      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At a pH above 7.5, the solubility of Mn decreases due to manganese precipitating as insoluble oxides and hydroxides as the soil becomes less acidic. This reduction in Mn availability directly leads to manganese deficiency symptoms in grapevines, which can manifest as interveinal chlorosis between the leaf veins and a general paling in the leaf's greens. More severe cases might result in brown necrotic spots or even the death of younger leaves. In grape leaves, manganese deficiency might also cause a distinctive V-shaped interveinal area along with a thinning white stripe that intersects the normal green tissue, a condition known as 'vertical leaf fleck disease' that can lead to reduced photosynthesis and consequent drop in fruit quality and yield. Monitoring leaves regularly for discoloration or stunted growth can aid in timely correction of soil pH extremes, ensuring optimal Mn uptake.",
